# 3. Every fixture is an adversarial self-test for one gate check. A fixture that is
#    never driven is a green light nobody earned.
#
# RUN IN PARALLEL, AND THE PREMISE THAT USED TO SIT HERE HAS EXPIRED. This block said for
# several releases that the suite is "latency-bound, not compute-bound -- most of its work is
# waiting on `git init`/commit/mktemp". That was measured over 83 fixtures at 72s and it is no
# longer true of this suite. Re-measured over 153 units: a full run is ~2480 CPU-seconds
# against a ~210s wall, i.e. roughly twelve cores busy for the whole run, and its heaviest
# units are fork-bound at 113% CPU with 62% of that in SYSTEM time.
#
# TWELVE, AND WIDER BUYS NOTHING. Swept on this box with the dispatched set pinned
# (AI_DLC_FIXTURE_NO_SKIP=1 on every run, the durations record reset from one golden copy
# before each, cells visited round-robin so drift spreads evenly rather than loading onto
# whichever ran last). Every run green; wall seconds, and the CPU-seconds the same runs cost:
#
#     -P8   [223.45, 276.22]   2346 CPU-s
#     -P12  [205.40, 218.75]   2433 CPU-s
#     -P16  [209.31, 213.89]   2483 CPU-s
#     -P20  [209.98, 213.76]   2495 CPU-s
#     -P24  [212.38, 220.75]   2500 CPU-s
#
# READ THE RANGES, NOT THE MEDIANS. A difference counts only where two cells' readings do not
# overlap, and on that rule exactly one comparison separates: -P8 is genuinely worse than the
# rest. TWELVE, SIXTEEN, TWENTY AND TWENTY-FOUR ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM EACH OTHER, and no
# number of further repetitions was going to separate them -- the spread WITHIN a cell is
# already the size of the differences BETWEEN them.
#
# -P8 IS THE CONTROL, NOT A CANDIDATE. It has to come out worse or the response variable is
# not measuring the pool at all, and every other cell is uninterpretable. It did.
#
# SO THE CHOICE INSIDE THAT BAND IS NOT A SPEED CLAIM. Wall clock is flat from 12 to 24 while
# CPU-seconds RISE MONOTONICALLY across the same range -- +67 from 12 to 24, all of it system
# time. Going wider buys no wall clock and spends contention for it. Twelve is the narrowest
# width in the indistinguishable band, so it is the one that leaves the most machine to
# everything else the operator is running. That is an operator's ruling about headroom, and it
# is recorded as one; the measurement establishes only that 12 is not slower.
#
# WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED HERE. That 12 beats 16 -- the ranges overlap and this does not claim
# it.
#
# THE INNER POOLS, SWEPT. ELEVEN fixtures open their own `xargs -P`, totalling 70 workers on top
# of this one. They cannot be varied by an environment variable -- `enforcement-map-sites/run.sh`
# scrubs every ambient AI_DLC_* name for I10, and I87 binds any key a shipped program
# dereferences -- so the sweep edited the eleven constants on an unpushed branch of a file://
# clone, one commit per cell, at this pool of 12, with the same design as above: dispatched set
# pinned by AI_DLC_FIXTURE_NO_SKIP=1, durations reset from one golden copy before every run, cells
# visited round-robin (A B A B C A C B C), three runs each. Wall seconds:
#
#     B  every inner pool at 1         [1179, 1323]
#     A  the constants as shipped      [1097, 1144]
#     C  every inner pool doubled      [1082, 1254]
#
# B IS THE CONTROL AND IT SEPARATED: its range does not overlap A's. Per unit it is not close --
# at width 1 a mutation battery ran 186-201s against 37-44s, consumer-machinery-home 249-258s
# against 66-67s, ledger-status-vocabulary 446-490s against 152-156s -- so the response variable
# does see the pools. A AND C OVERLAP, so doubling every pool buys no wall clock this sweep can
# resolve. The suite stays bound by `reconcile-emit-report`, which opens no inner pool and was the
# longest unit in all nine runs. The constants stay as they are: they are the narrowest setting in
# the indistinguishable band, which leaves the most machine to everything else, the same ruling
# as the outer pool's. Per-unit costs are recorded in BL-004's archived entry.
#
# THE NUMBER IS MEASURED ON ONE MACHINE. That is why it stays the tunable
# AI_DLC_FIXTURE_JOBS: on a smaller box, re-measure rather than inherit this.
#
# THE BLOCK BELOW IS JOINED TO core/git-hooks/pre-push BY I66, and the join is on
# EXECUTABLE LINES ONLY. For this project's whole history the distribution ran its
# suite through a pool with a completeness assertion and an empty-suite guard while
# shipping consumers a serial loop that returned 0 when it ran nothing -- 313 lines
# here against 208 there, and nothing compared them. Prose stays deliberately
# different (this one addresses a maintainer, that one a consumer, and the pool size
# is re-derived per box); the code must not. Comments are stripped before the
# comparison because a comparison a comment can satisfy is I64's defect: the token
# is present and the mechanism is absent.
# FIXTURE_POOL_BEGIN
FIXTURE_JOBS="${AI_DLC_FIXTURE_JOBS:-12}"
# THE FIXTURE ROOT, SPELLED ONCE AND SLASH-TERMINATED. It is the one line I66 maps between the hooks.
FXROOT="core/fixtures/"
# RECURSION BOUND. A fixture that drives a hook runs this pool again, and nothing else limits the
# nesting. The depth marker is deliberately NOT named AI_DLC_*: fixtures scrub that whole prefix from
# their environment before driving a hook, so a marker carrying it would be removed exactly where it
# has to survive. Depth 1 is a hook started under a pool's fixture, and its pool is narrowed to two
# workers, not one, because a fixture that asserts parallelism needs two in flight. Depth 2 or more
# refuses to open a pool (run_fixtures). No AI_DLC_FIXTURE_JOBS value lifts the depth-1 cap: one or two
# is kept, and anything else -- larger, zero (BSD `xargs -P 0` is UNLIMITED), not a number, or longer
# than five digits -- becomes two. A drive under `env -i` clears the marker with everything else and must
# pass the depth explicitly. A marker that is SET but not a number, or longer than five digits, is depth
# 2 and refuses: it FAILS CLOSED, because bash arithmetic wraps a 20-digit value (2^64 reads as 0) and
# `[` errors on one, either of which would otherwise open a full pool. The `10#` reads 08 as eight.
POOL_DEPTH_IN="${PREPUSH_POOL_DEPTH:-0}"
case "$POOL_DEPTH_IN" in *[!0-9]*|??????*) POOL_DEPTH_IN=2 ;; esac
POOL_DEPTH_IN=$((10#$POOL_DEPTH_IN))
if [ "$POOL_DEPTH_IN" -ge 1 ]; then
  case "$FIXTURE_JOBS" in ''|*[!0-9]*|??????*) FIXTURE_JOBS=2 ;; esac
  if [ "$FIXTURE_JOBS" -lt 1 ] || [ "$FIXTURE_JOBS" -gt 2 ]; then FIXTURE_JOBS=2; fi
fi
# A UNIT THAT MAKES NO PROGRESS IS REFUSED, NEVER JUDGED ON ELAPSED TIME (BL-606). Every unit runs through
# the worker's `pool_run`, which starts it in its own process group with TMPDIR set to a per-unit WATCHED
# ROOT, a fresh `mktemp -d "$out/.t.XXXXX"` under a short "$out" `<root>/fx.XXXXXX` (never the fixture name; the
# root order is at run_fixtures), and polls once a second.
# PROGRESS IS NEW BYTES, AND NOTHING ELSE: the summed size of every file under the watched root plus the
# unit's pool log. CPU time and process membership are not in the signature. The watched root holds the
# hermetic runner's work dir (its HR_LOG and sandbox) and every template-based `mktemp`. A poll that
# sees the same byte count as the last one is still; POOL_STALL consecutive still polls are a refusal.
# Measured, the reasons for that definition:
#   - CPU: a `while :; do sleep 1; done` loop shell's CPU time moved on only 20 of 330 one-second polls
#     (its longest unchanged run 18), and its fresh `sleep` pid changes membership every poll, so a
#     signature carrying either never refuses a polling hang; separating that from real work would take
#     a tuned CPU threshold.
#   - log only: hermetic-run.sh:538-541 buffers the fixture's output in HR_LOG and writes the pool log
#     only at exit, so a declared unit's pool log is empty for its whole run.
#   - the watched root's limit: 77 of 307 fixture run.sh files use a bare `mktemp -d`, and `mktemp -t`
#     likewise, which on macOS ignore TMPDIR; their scratch writes are NOT seen. A unit whose only work
#     is silent computation, or writes outside the root, is refused, by design.
#   - a NESTED POOL's root (a unit that drives this block again) is `/tmp/fx.XXXXXX`, outside the watched
#     root, because no unit TMPDIR passes the 35-encoded root rule (BL-691). So the watched root is
#     DISCOVERABLE: pool_run writes an empty `.pool-unit` marker into it, and a nested run_fixtures walks up
#     from its physical TMPDIR to the nearest `.t.?????` directory it owns that carries one, writes that
#     directory into its own root as `.pool-parent`, and puts its root FIRST in the marker directory's
#     `.pool-nested` (pruned of removed roots on every rewrite, and never through a symlink), so the newest
#     root is inside the 16 lines the watcher reads each poll. It walks an entry only when it is a directory,
#     not a symlink, named `fx.??????`, and its `.pool-parent` is a regular file (a FIFO would block the
#     `read`, and the watcher with it) naming this watched root. The unit under test writes the list as
#     the same uid, so this is a consistency check, not a security boundary. A nested pool that fell back to a bare
#     `mktemp -d` root is not named `fx.??????` and stays unwatched.
# AT THE IN-POOL TRACE SITE A THIRD PATH IS WATCHED, `wx`: the traced fixture's own output file, which the
# deriver writes as `$WORK/<fx>.log` with WORK="$AI_DLC_READSET_TRACE_ROOT/w". That trace root lives under
# the hook's TMPDIR, outside the watched root, and the pool log there gets only the deriver's say-lines, so
# without `wx` a traced fixture whose only progress is output was refused where its plain run was ok. ONLY
# the .log: the same dir's `<fx>.raw` is the `log stream` capture of the fixture's sandbox REPORTS (one per
# file operation under the tree, plus stream notices), so a hang that stats one file a second would count
# as progress forever. `wx` is empty at every other site. This path is the deriver's layout, and
# core/fixtures/consumer-suite-pool checks the deriver still writes it there.
# On a refusal the pool kills every process in the unit's tree and every group found in it -- the tree
# is walked from the recorded pid by parent link AND by every process group in it, because units start
# their own groups -- by recorded id, never by pattern. The verdict is REFUSED: no recorded duration, no
# recorded key, no kept trace rows, and the push exits 2 rather than 1 when nothing else failed. The
# window is counted in POLLS, so a loaded box widens it instead of shrinking it.
# A refusal costs a re-run, never a verdict. The default, POOL_STALL_DEFAULT, is set from the measurement
# this pool instruments: each run publishes every unit's LONGEST silent stretch (consecutive polls with no
# new bytes) to "$GITDIR/ai-dlc-pool-stall.last", and one forced full run with a very high window, on an
# unpushed throwaway branch inside a slot, produces the false-positive set and the figure together. The
# default is that measured maximum plus a stated margin. The figures -- the maximum, the margin, and the
# units nearest it -- are recorded in the BL-606 backlog entry and the CHANGELOG, not here.
# AI_DLC_POOL_STALL overrides it, for a fixture driving a deliberately hung unit.
POOL_STALL_DEFAULT=600                     # D9-WINDOW
POOL_STALL="${AI_DLC_POOL_STALL:-$POOL_STALL_DEFAULT}"
case "$POOL_STALL" in ''|*[!0-9]*|??????*|0|00|000|0000|00000) [ -n "${AI_DLC_POOL_STALL:-}" ] && printf 'pre-push: AI_DLC_POOL_STALL=%s rejected (want 1-99999); using the default %s\n' "$AI_DLC_POOL_STALL" "$POOL_STALL_DEFAULT" >&2; POOL_STALL=$POOL_STALL_DEFAULT ;; esac
POOL_STALL=$((10#$POOL_STALL))
POOL_REFUSED_ONLY=0
# THE TREE WALK, one `ps` and one awk per poll. Input rows are `pid ppid pgid stat time`; `root` is the
# unit's recorded pid, `self` the worker's own group (never walked, never signalled: a unit whose `set -m`
# did not take is still walked by parent link, and the hook's own group is never killed), `groups` the
# groups the last poll saw (so a grandchild reparented to init stays reachable through its group), `known`
# the pids the last poll saw. AN EMPTY `self` (its `ps` failed) WALKS NOTHING: pool_kill then kills the
# recorded pid alone, because `pg[i] != self` excludes no group and a unit whose `set -m` also failed
# shares the push's foreground group. A SAVED GROUP IS TAKEN ONLY WHILE IT STILL HOLDS A KNOWN PID: a group whose
# members are all gone may have a reused id naming an unrelated process, and it is never walked or killed.
# Output is one line `<S|U>|<signature>|<live pids>|<live groups>`; U means the root row was not visible.
POOL_WALK='BEGIN { n = split(groups, g0, " "); for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) SG[g0[i]] = 1
  n = split(known, k0, " "); for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) K[k0[i]] = 1; K[root] = 1 }
{ pid[NR] = $1; pp[NR] = $2; pg[NR] = $3; st[NR] = $4; tm[NR] = $5 }
END {
  for (i = 1; i <= NR; i++) if ((pg[i] in SG) && (pid[i] in K)) G[pg[i]] = 1
  T[root] = 1
  do {
    c = 0
    for (i = 1; i <= NR; i++) {
      if (pid[i] in T) {
        if (pg[i] != self && pg[i] > 1 && !(pg[i] in G)) { G[pg[i]] = 1; c = 1 }
      } else if ((pp[i] in T) || (pg[i] in G)) { T[pid[i]] = 1; c = 1 }
    }
  } while (c)
  seen = "U"; sig = ""; lp = ""; lg = ""
  for (i = 1; i <= NR; i++) {
    if (!(pid[i] in T)) continue
    if (pid[i] == root) seen = "S"
    sig = sig pid[i] ":" pg[i] ":" tm[i] ","
    if (st[i] !~ /^Z/) { lp = lp " " pid[i]; if (pg[i] != self && pg[i] > 1) H[pg[i]] = 1 }
  }
  for (k in H) lg = lg " " k
  printf "%s|%s|%s|%s\n", seen, sig, lp, lg
}'
# RESOLVE THE GIT DIRECTORY; NEVER SPELL IT `.git/`. In a LINKED WORKTREE `.git` is a
# FILE, not a directory, so every record below fails to open -- and each write is
# error-suppressed, so the suite proceeds and the banner is unaffected while the
# cross-run evidence is silently lost. `--git-common-dir` answers `.git` in a primary
# checkout and the shared admin directory from a worktree, so one spelling is correct
# in both. The fallback keeps the old behaviour if rev-parse is unavailable.
GITDIR="$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)" || GITDIR=".git"
[ -n "$GITDIR" ] || GITDIR=".git"
DURATIONS_RECORD="$GITDIR/ai-dlc-fixture-durations"
# THIS RUN'S COSTS, UNMERGED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE FROM THE RECORD ABOVE.
# DURATIONS_RECORD merges each run over the last one (a red run under `<unit>@red` keys) so a SKIPPED unit keeps the cost it had --
# correct for scheduling, useless for measurement. Its rows are of mixed vintage and nothing in
# it says which run wrote which, so reading a pole figure off it compares two different days.
# This file holds only what THIS pool actually timed.
#
# TRUNCATED AT POOL ENTRY, so the file cannot outlive the run that wrote it. A reader finding it
# non-empty has a measurement from a pool that entered, dispatched, and came back green; every
# other path -- red unit, dead worker, mktemp failure, the empty-suite guard -- leaves it EMPTY,
# which is the state a reader must treat as "nothing was measured" rather than as a figure.
#
# PUBLISHED ONLY ON A FULLY GREEN RUN OVER THE DISPATCHED LIST, gated exactly like
# VERIFIED_RECORD below and for the same reason: a red run's timings are the timings of a suite
# that did not finish, and a guard comparing them against a full-suite baseline would read a
# short run as an improvement.
#
# THE CONTENT-KEY SKIP NEVER ENTERS THE POOL, so it neither truncates nor writes -- the PRIOR
# run's file survives it. That is why the reader is the hook step, which stands down on
# SUITE_SKIPPED rather than trusting the file's age.
LASTRUN_RECORD="$GITDIR/ai-dlc-fixture-durations.last"
READSET_MAP=".ai-dlc-fixture-readsets.tsv"
# THE VERIFIED-STATE RECORD (VERIFIED_RECORD, its format sidecar VERIFIED_FORMAT), THE PER-FIXTURE KEY RECORDS
# (KEYS_DIR) AND THE CONSUMER-LAYOUT KEY RECORDS (CL_KEYS_DIR) live in the shared verdict store base, outside the
# working tree the content key hashes; the RECORD_HOME span (after READSET_VS) resolves them.
# A RED UNIT'S OUTPUT HAS TO OUTLIVE THE RUN THAT PRODUCED IT. Until this record existed the
# worker below sent every fixture's stdout and stderr to /dev/null, so an intermittent red left
# ONE line of evidence -- `FAIL <name>` in a terminal -- and the honest local remedy was "push
# again", which is indistinguishable from bypassing a real failure. This repo has spent at least
# three release cycles proposing and refuting causes for one such intermittent, every
# investigation re-running the fixture out of band because the gate that SAW the failure kept
# nothing. Filed by the graph consumer as
# PC-S302-FIXTURE-SUITE-POOL-PRODUCES-AN-UNREPRODUCIBLE-FAIL-AND-THE-EVIDENCE-IS-DELETED-WITH-THE-TEMP-DIR.
#
# UNDER .git FOR THE SAME REASON AS THE TWO ABOVE: the content key hashes the working tree, so a
# record written into it would change the key that decides whether this suite runs at all.
#
# NEVER DELETED ON A GREEN RUN, and that is the whole point. The workflow this exists to serve is
# "it went red, I pushed again, it went green" -- a green run that cleaned up would destroy the
# evidence at exactly the moment the operator went looking for it. The record names the run that
# wrote it, and nothing points a reader at it unless THIS run is red.
#
# AND IT IS OVERWRITTEN BY THE NEXT RED RUN, so every red run also leaves its own copy:
# `ai-dlc-fixture-failures.<UTC %Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ>.<pid>`, pruned to the newest FAILLOG_KEEP of that
# shape. Measured at batch 179: one intermittent red's got-vector was the only evidence of its
# cause, and it lived in a file the next red run would have replaced. The prune takes a name only
# if it starts with a year and month AND its last dot-field is all digits, so a record saved by
# hand is safe when its name fails either test -- `.clean` fails the first, a retained copy
# renamed with a `.keep` suffix fails the second. A hand-saved name passing both is not.
FAILLOG_RECORD="$GITDIR/ai-dlc-fixture-failures"
FAILLOG_KEEP=20
# EVERY RECORD ABOVE IS SHARED BY CONCURRENT PUSHES: the common dir serves every worktree, and two
# pushes may run at once. A record is therefore REPLACED BY RENAME and never written in place, so a
# reader sees the old file or the new one whole, never a torn one. rec_put stages the copy beside
# the record under this process's pid, then renames it over the record.
rec_put() { # <src> <record>
  { [ -d "${2%/*}" ] || mkdir -p "${2%/*}" 2>/dev/null; } && cp "$1" "$2.tmp.$$" 2>/dev/null && mv -f "$2.tmp.$$" "$2" 2>/dev/null && return 0
  rm -f "$2.tmp.$$" 2>/dev/null
  return 1
}
# A READ-MODIFY-WRITE OF A SHARED RECORD runs under a mkdir lock judged by the live-trace lock's own
# staleness test (readset_lock_stale), so a push killed while holding it frees it as soon as its pid
# is gone. Its pid file is `<pid> <epoch>`, written by rename. Never waits: a held lock returns 1 and
# the caller skips its write and says so.
rec_lock() { # <lock dir>
  if ! mkdir "$1" 2>/dev/null; then
    readset_lock_stale "$1" && rm -rf "$1" 2>/dev/null
    mkdir "$1" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  fi
  { printf '%s %s\n' "$$" "$(date +%s)" > "$1/pid.tmp.$$" && mv -f "$1/pid.tmp.$$" "$1/pid"; } 2>/dev/null || rm -f "$1/pid.tmp.$$" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}
rec_unlock() { rm -rf "$1" 2>/dev/null; }

# Reduce "$1" (the dispatch list) to the fixtures whose KEY moved. Each fixture F has a key
# record, `$KEYS_DIR/<F>.key`, holding the PRE-RUN hashes of the last run F passed on. F is
# skipped iff its record exists and every key in K(F) still matches; otherwise F runs.
#
# K(F): every path in F's read set (its committed rows, or its valid local rows once a trace of
# it lands -- those are ADDED to the committed rows, never shadow them), plus every file under F's own directory, each
# keyed by its `.now` sha or `-` when absent; the entry list of every DIRECTORY in that set,
# derived from the hashed path list and never from readdir; and the tool fingerprint. A fixture
# with no rows at all is keyed on the whole `.now` universe.
#
# NOT KEYED, the same exposure as before this record existed: `.git/**`, the content key's
# EXCLUDE tops (readset_excluded_re), git-ignored paths, anything read under `$HOME`, and
# environment knobs.
#
# A record is written per fixture on PASS, whatever its neighbours did; never on a fail, a kill
# or a missing verdict. A fixture that ran because a FILE in its read set or own directory moved
# is recorded `stale`: a stale fixture always runs. An UNDECLARED stale fixture is taken by the post-green
# live trace; once that trace lands its local rows are its key and the next pass records it `ok`. A stale
# DECLARED fixture (one with an `inputs.decl`) is mapped before the trace queue and the live trace skips
# it, so it is re-run and never traced; trace it by hand with `--declared-out`. A LISTING
# change never stales: the record is written `ok` with every file and directory under each
# newly appeared entry added to its keys, so whatever F might now read there is keyed.
#
# The ONLY run-all is an empty `.now`: the hashing instrument is broken. NO_SKIP stays.
# Hash every path whose change could matter, into "$1/.now" as `path<TAB>sha`.
#
# THE SUBJECT IS TRACKED FILES *PLUS* EVERY PATH THE MAP NAMES. Tracked files are in it so a
# change to something NO fixture is known to read is still SEEN -- otherwise a validator that
# started reading a new file would drift out of the map with nothing able to notice. Measured
# on this tree: 1% of changed files across 40 commits fell outside every read-set, and that
#1% is exactly the population this arm exists for.
#
# `.git/**` IS DELIBERATELY OUT OF SCOPE, and it is a measurement rather than a convenience.
# Fixtures read git internals constantly -- 33 of 118 name some `.git` path, because validators
# they invoke resolve a root or ask `git status`. But `.git/HEAD`, `.git/index` and the refs
# move on EVERY commit, so honouring them would select those fixtures on every push and take
# the suite-wide win from 42% down to 12%, with two of the heaviest units running every time.
# Measured before excluding them: five fixtures read git HISTORY pointers (refs/HEAD/logs), and
# running all five across an empty commit -- HEAD moved, tree byte-identical -- changed NOT ONE
# verdict, with controls proving HEAD really moved and that the comparison could detect a
# difference. Their git reads are incidental to their verdicts. Excluded here rather than in the
# map so the map stays a faithful record of what was actually read.
# THE CONTENT KEY'S EXCLUSION SET, RESOLVED RATHER THAN RESTATED.
#
# `scripts/suite-content-key.sh` declares which paths cannot change a fixture verdict, and it
# earns each line with a two-tree mutation measurement. The OUTER skip honours that set; this
# INNER one did not, and the asymmetry cost a full suite on almost every push here. A changed
# path in no fixture read-set forces all N, and a NEW file is in no read-set by construction --
# the map is a generated snapshot, so it cannot name a file that did not exist when it was
# derived. `docs/plans/` is edited in nearly every session on this repo, so the arm that exists
# for a genuinely unknown dependency was firing on a directory the key already declares
# irrelevant. Measured: one plan file forced all 133 fixtures on a push whose subject was a
# one-line change.
#
# RESOLVED FROM THE DECLARATION, so this is not a second copy of the list. When the declaring
# script is absent -- it is distribution-only, and a consumer does not have it -- the set is
# EMPTY and this reduces to the previous behaviour exactly. Both hooks run this same code and
# I66 compares them on executable lines.
#
# `.git` is dropped here because it is already excluded, separately and on different grounds,
# at both of the sites below.
#
# THE SENTINELS BELOW BOUND THE HOOK'S UNIVERSE, and derive-fixture-readsets.sh SOURCES this span
# out of the runner it reads its fixture root from. The digest it writes for a traced fixture and
# the digest this hook recomputes to clear a stale record are the SAME function over the SAME
# universe filter, so the two sides cannot drift; `--reconcile` reads the key records through the
# same parse. Nothing in the span reads a global but READSET_MAP and READSET_LOCAL, which the
# deriver binds before it calls readset_manifest.
# READSET_UNIVERSE_BEGIN
readset_excluded_re() {
  local decl="scripts/suite-content-key.sh" tops t re=""
  [ -f "$decl" ] || return 0
  tops="$(sed -n '/^# EXCLUDE_BEGIN$/,/^# EXCLUDE_END$/p' "$decl" \
          | sed -n 's/^\([A-Za-z0-9._-]\{1,\}\)$/\1/p' | grep -v '^\.git$')"
  [ -n "$tops" ] || return 0
  for t in $tops; do
    re="${re}|^${t//./\\.}(/|$)"
  done
  printf '%s' "${re#|}"
}

# Drop every path the exclusion set covers. A no-op when the set is empty -- which is the
# consumer case, and must stay a no-op there rather than filtering on an empty pattern, since
# `grep -vE ""` matches everything and would empty the stream.
readset_drop_excluded() { # reads stdin, writes stdout
  local re; re="$(readset_excluded_re)"
  if [ -n "$re" ]; then grep -vE "$re"; else cat; fi
}

# Copy <in> to <res> minus every row whose first tab field git reports IGNORED. ONE helper for
# both sites that build a path set NOW -- the manifest and the orphan universe -- because they
# must agree or a path dropped from one reads as changed in the other. `check-ignore` in its
# default mode never reports a TRACKED file, so a force-added file that matches an ignore
# pattern stays in; a `.gitignore` grep would drop it. That holds only while the file is STILL
# tracked, which is why a verified RECORD is never passed here, stamped or not: once `git rm`'d
# such a file is untracked and ignored, and filtering the record would erase its vanish.
# Exit 1 means "nothing
# ignored". Any other exit fails CLOSED: <res> is emptied and the caller runs everything.
# A PATH INSIDE A SUBMODULE IS NEVER ASKED, because one such path makes `check-ignore` die with
# 128 ("Pathspec ... is in submodule") and that would fail closed on EVERY push. Measured on the
# reference consumer: 397 of its 21034 map paths sit under its one gitlink. They are kept --
# the superproject's ignore rules do not reach inside a submodule.
readset_drop_ignored() { # <in> <res>
  local in="$1" res="$2" ci_rc
  git -c core.quotePath=false ls-files -s 2>/dev/null \
    | awk '$1 == "160000" { sub(/^[^\t]*\t/, ""); print }' > "$res.gl"
  cut -f1 "$in" | awk -v gl="$res.gl" 'BEGIN { while ((getline l < gl) > 0) g[++n] = l "/" }
    { for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) if (index($0, g[i]) == 1) next; print }' > "$res.q"
  git -c core.quotePath=false check-ignore --stdin < "$res.q" > "$res.ign" 2>/dev/null
  ci_rc=$?
  if [ "$ci_rc" -ne 0 ] && [ "$ci_rc" -ne 1 ]; then : > "$res"; return 1; fi
  awk -F'\t' -v ign="$res.ign" 'BEGIN { while ((getline l < ign) > 0) drop[l] = 1 }
                                !($1 in drop)' "$in" > "$res"
}

# ONE HELPER EMITS THE MERGED ROW SET, AND EVERY SITE THAT READ THE COMMITTED MAP CALLS IT. A missed
# site turns a local-only path into an orphan and forces the full suite, or leaves a locally-mapped
# fixture "unmapped" on one side of a join and mapped on the other.
#   paths  committed paths plus EVERY local candidate path -- the manifest's universe. Validity is
#          NOT applied here: the manifest is what validity is computed FROM.
#   rows   `<fx>\t<path>` for selection. A fixture with VALID local rows (`$out/.local.valid`)
#          uses only those; every other fixture uses its committed rows.
# The local rows are read FIRST, so the committed rows of a locally-traced fixture are dropped.
# Every `#` line of the committed map -- the header and each `# digest` line -- is dropped by the
# `grep -v` before it can reach a row set.
readset_rows() { # <out> <paths|rows>
  local out="$1" mode="$2"
  { if [ "$mode" = rows ]; then [ -s "$out/.local.valid" ] && sed 's/^/V	/' "$out/.local.valid"
    else [ -s "$READSET_LOCAL" ] && sed 's/^/L	/' "$READSET_LOCAL"; fi
    [ -s "$READSET_MAP" ] && grep -v '^#' "$READSET_MAP" | sed 's/^/C	/'
    :
  } | awk -F'\t' -v mode="$mode" '
    $1 == "V" { loc[$2] = 1; print $2 "\t" $3; next }
    $1 == "L" { if (substr($3, 1, 1) != "#") print $3; next }
    $1 == "C" { if (mode != "rows") print $3; else print $2 "\t" $3 }'
}

# THE UNIVERSE FILTER ON A PATH STREAM: `.git` and `.git/**`, then the content key's excluded tops.
# The manifest and the committed digest both call it, so the two cannot disagree on which paths count.
readset_drop_git() { # reads paths on stdin, writes all but `.git` and `.git/**`; awk, so an all-`.git` stream exits 0
  awk '$0 != ".git" && index($0, ".git/") != 1'
}
readset_universe_paths() { # reads paths on stdin, writes the ones in the universe
  readset_drop_git | readset_drop_excluded
}

readset_manifest() {
  local out="$1" hash_rc ign_rc
  # UNTRACKED-BUT-UNIGNORED FILES ARE IN THE UNIVERSE, AND THE WHOLE-SUITE SKIP BELOW IS WHY.
  # `git ls-files` alone answers about the COMMITTED tree, which is the right population for
  # "what am I pushing" and the wrong one for "what will a fixture read": a fixture reads the
  # WORKING tree, and an untracked file there is one a trace never saw and no read-set names.
  # Without this line `.changed` could be empty while an unignored file sat beside a fixture's
  # subject, and the no-change branch would skip the suite over a tree nobody hashed.
  # Git-ignored paths stay out, for the reason the content key states about the same set:
  # hooks and fixtures WRITE INTO them while the suite runs, so a universe covering them would
  # differ from itself across the very run it is keyed on.
  # THE MAP ARM USED TO BYPASS THAT, and it is dropped here from EVERY source. `--exclude-standard`
  # filters only the untracked listing; a map row naming an ignored file was hashed whenever the
  # file existed. Measured: a trace run in the main checkout recorded three ignored `.DS_Store`
  # files, a green run there wrote them into the verified record in the SHARED git dir, and a
  # push from a worktree without them saw them VANISH -- their parents, `core` among them, joined
  # `.match`, and 11 changed paths selected 226 of 232 fixtures where their own rows select 55.
  #
  # A MANIFEST THAT HASHED LESS THAN IT LISTED IS EMPTIED, so the guard in apply_readset_skip
  # runs everything. `xargs` without `-0` stops at the first apostrophe in a filename and the
  # files after it were never hashed -- measured on the reference consumer, 9600 of 12561, so an
  # edit to any later path read as "nothing changed" and skipped the whole suite. A name git
  # still has to quote (tab, newline, `"` or `\`) cannot be hashed by its listed spelling, and
  # an unreadable file fails shasum; both empty it too, so such a file runs the whole suite on
  # every push until it is renamed or made readable. `--` ends shasum's options, so a top-level
  # name beginning with `-` is hashed as a file rather than refused as an option.
  # THE QUOTED-PATH GUARD READS THE TWO ls-files STREAMS ONLY (`.lsf`): a map row is a spelling someone
  # wrote, not a name git had to quote, and one quoted row in a local map used to empty `.now` for the whole
  # push. A quoted MAP row is dropped from the universe here and its fixture is forced to run in
  # readset_keys (`.qfx`), so it fails closed for that fixture alone. `.now.why` names the first reason `.now` is emptied.
  { git -c core.quotePath=false ls-files 2>/dev/null
    git -c core.quotePath=false ls-files --others --exclude-standard 2>/dev/null
  } > "$out/.lsf"
  rm -f "$out/.now.why"
  { cat "$out/.lsf"
    readset_rows "$out" paths | grep -v '^"'
    :
  } | readset_universe_paths | sort -u > "$out/.paths.all"
  readset_drop_ignored "$out/.paths.all" "$out/.paths"; ign_rc=$?
  # `.modes` (BL-1155): `<path>\t<tag>` for each admitted path that is not a plain regular file, `x` for an executable
  # one and `l:<target>` for a symlink, so a mode or type change reaches the key decision. Builtins, and one readlink
  # per symlink.
  while IFS= read -r p; do [ -f "$p" ] || continue; printf '%s\n' "$p"
    if [ -L "$p" ]; then printf '%s\tl:%s\n' "$p" "$(readlink "$p")" >&3; elif [ -x "$p" ]; then printf '%s\tx\n' "$p" >&3; fi
  done < "$out/.paths" > "$out/.files" 3> "$out/.modes"
  tr '\n' '\000' < "$out/.files" | xargs -0 -n 200 shasum -a 256 -- > "$out/.sums" 2>/dev/null
  hash_rc=$?
  awk '{ path = $0; sub(/^[0-9a-f]+  /, "", path); print path "\t" $1 }' "$out/.sums" | sort > "$out/.now"
  if [ "$hash_rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'shasum failed (exit %s)\n' "$hash_rc" > "$out/.now.why"
  elif [ "$ign_rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'the git-ignore filter failed (exit %s)\n' "$ign_rc" > "$out/.now.why"
  elif grep -q '^"' "$out/.lsf"; then printf 'git quotes the path %s (from git ls-files; rename it)\n' "$(grep -m1 '^"' "$out/.lsf")" > "$out/.now.why"
  elif [ "$(wc -l < "$out/.now")" -ne "$(wc -l < "$out/.files")" ]; then printf 'hashed %s of %s listed files (an unreadable file or a name xargs split)\n' "$(wc -l < "$out/.now" | tr -d ' ')" "$(wc -l < "$out/.files" | tr -d ' ')" > "$out/.now.why"
  fi
  if [ -s "$out/.now.why" ]; then : > "$out/.now"; fi
}

# THE KEY RECORDS, ONE PARSE: `<fx>\t#state\t<state>`, `<fx>\t#tools\t<tools format>` and
# `<fx>\t<key>\t<value>` for every record under <keys dir> whose first line is `#format k1`. readset_keys
# and the deriver's `--reconcile` both read it. `#tools canonical` marks tool rows resolved against the
# fixed tool dirs; any other value, or none, is a record written by the PATH-resolved hook. `<fx>\t#pending\t<n>`
# carries line 4, the PENDING row count of the pass the record was written on; a record without it predates the count.
# The value is passed through as written: it only rides into `.kpn`, and every reader of that (readset_keys_write,
# readset_pending_recorded) keeps a non-negative integer alone, so a malformed `#pending` line reads as unrecorded.
readset_key_records() { # <keys dir>
  awk 'FNR == 1 { ok = ($0 == "#format k1"); f = FILENAME; sub(/.*\//, "", f); sub(/\.key$/, "", f); next }
       !ok { next }
       /^#state / { print f "\t#state\t" substr($0, 8); next }
       /^#tools / { print f "\t#tools\t" substr($0, 8); next }
       /^#pending / { print f "\t#pending\t" substr($0, 10); next }
       /^#modes / { print f "\t#modes\t" substr($0, 8); next }
       /^#mode\t/ { print f "\t" $0; next }
       /^#/ { next }
       { print f "\t" $0 }' "$1"/*.key 2>/dev/null
}

# THE READ-SET DIGEST: per fixture, the sha256 of its C-sorted `<path>\t<sha>` lines, kept to the paths in
# <keep> and never <skip>. ONE function, called by the deriver when it writes `# digest <fx> <sha>` into
# the committed map and by this hook when it recomputes that digest over this push's `.now`.
#   <rows>  `<fx>\t<path>\t<sha256 or ->`   <keep>  the universe, one path per line
#   <skip>  the committed map's own path: a fixture that reads the map would otherwise carry the sha of
#           the map BEFORE its digest line was written, and could never match again
#   <dir>   a scratch directory that must not exist yet
# Prints `<fx>\t<digest>`. A fixture with no kept row gets no digest, which never matches one.
readset_digests() { # <rows> <keep> <skip> <dir>
  mkdir "$4" || return 1
  RD_K="$2" awk -F'\t' -v skip="$3" 'FILENAME == ENVIRON["RD_K"] { k[$0] = 1; next }
    ($2 in k) && $2 != skip { print $1 "\t" $2 "\t" $3 }' "$2" "$1" | LC_ALL=C sort -u \
    | awk -F'\t' -v d="$4" '$1 != f { if (f != "") close(o); f = $1; o = d "/" f } { print $2 "\t" $3 > o }' || return 1
  find "$4" -type f -print0 | xargs -0 shasum -a 256 -- 2>/dev/null \
    | awk '{ f = $0; sub(/^[0-9a-f]+  /, "", f); sub(/.*\//, "", f); print f "\t" $1 }'
}

# THE COMMITTED DIGESTS, CHECKED AGAINST THIS TREE. Writes `$out/.cdig`, one `<fx>\t<status>` per
# fixture with committed rows: `match`, `differs`, or `nodigest` (rows and no `# digest <fx> <sha>`
# line -- a map written before digests existed). The recomputed digest reads each committed path's sha
# off `.now`, `-` where no file of that name is in it, kept to `.paths` -- the universe the manifest
# hashed. NO DERIVER SHA: a committed row set is judged by its content alone, and the deriver sha stays
# a criterion of the local map only. Only `match` clears; `nodigest` rows still select their fixture.
readset_committed_digests() { # <out>
  local out="$1"
  : > "$out/.cdig"
  [ -s "$READSET_MAP" ] && [ -s "$out/.now" ] || return 0
  awk '$1 == "#" && $2 == "digest" && NF == 4 { print $3 "\t" $4 }' "$READSET_MAP" > "$out/.cdig.want"
  RC_W="$out/.cdig.want" RC_N="$out/.now" awk -F'\t' '
    FILENAME == ENVIRON["RC_W"] { w[$1] = 1; next }
    FILENAME == ENVIRON["RC_N"] { now[$1] = $2; next }
    /^#/ { next }
    ($1 in w) { print $1 "\t" $2 "\t" (($2 in now) ? now[$2] : "-") }' "$out/.cdig.want" "$out/.now" "$READSET_MAP" > "$out/.cdig.rows"
  : > "$out/.cdig.got"
  [ -s "$out/.cdig.rows" ] && { readset_dir_values "$out/.cdig.rows" "$out/.now" "$out/.paths" "$out/.cdig.rows2" && readset_digests "$out/.cdig.rows2" "$out/.cdig.rows2.keep" "$READSET_MAP" "$out/.dg" > "$out/.cdig.got" || : > "$out/.cdig.got"; }
  RC_W="$out/.cdig.want" RC_G="$out/.cdig.got" awk -F'\t' '
    FILENAME == ENVIRON["RC_W"] { w[$1] = $2; next }
    FILENAME == ENVIRON["RC_G"] { g[$1] = $2; next }
    /^#/ || $1 == "" { next }
    { c[$1] = 1 }
    END { for (f in c) print f "\t" (!(f in w) ? "nodigest" : (((f in g) && g[f] == w[f]) ? "match" : "differs")) }' \
    "$out/.cdig.want" "$out/.cdig.got" "$READSET_MAP" | LC_ALL=C sort > "$out/.cdig"
}
# `<dir>/\t#listing:<sha256>` for every directory a path list implies, derived from the list alone and
# never from readdir. A listing is the directory's immediate entry names, C-sorted, one per line.
readset_listings() { # <path list> <out file>
  local w="$2.d"
  mkdir -p "$w" || { : > "$2"; return 1; }
  awk '{ p = $0; while ((i = match(p, /\/[^\/]*$/)) > 0) { d = substr(p, 1, i - 1); print d "\t" substr(p, i + 1); p = d } }' "$1" \
    | LC_ALL=C sort -u | awk -F'\t' -v w="$w" '
      $1 != d { if (f != "") close(f); d = $1; n++; f = w "/" n; print n "\t" d "/" > (w "/.idx") }
      { print $2 > f }'
  find "$w" -type f ! -name .idx -print0 | xargs -0 shasum -a 256 -- 2>/dev/null \
    | awk -v idx="$w/.idx" 'BEGIN { while ((getline l < idx) > 0) { t = index(l, "\t"); d[substr(l, 1, t - 1)] = substr(l, t + 1) } }
      { n = $0; sub(/^[0-9a-f]+  /, "", n); sub(/.*\//, "", n); if (n in d) print d[n] "\t#listing:" $1 }' > "$2"
}

# A DIRECTORY ROW'S DIGEST VALUE IS ITS LISTING, NEVER `-`. `<rows>` is `<fx>\t<path>\t<value>`; a row whose
# value is `-` and whose path names a directory <now> implies takes that directory's `#listing:<sha>` and its
# path joins the keep list (the universe holds files only, so readset_digests would otherwise drop the row),
# so a committed digest sees an entry added to or removed from a directory a fixture reads. The deriver
# (writing the digest) and the hook (recomputing it) both call this, so the two cannot disagree.
# Writes the rows to <out> and the widened keep list to <out>.keep.
readset_dir_values() { # <rows> <now> <paths> <out>
  local w="$4.ls"
  cut -f1 "$2" > "$4.p" || return 1
  readset_listings "$4.p" "$w" || return 1
  RV_L="$w" RV_P="$3" RV_K="$4.keep" awk -F'\t' '
    FILENAME == ENVIRON["RV_L"] { l[$1] = $2; next }
    FILENAME == ENVIRON["RV_P"] { print > ENVIRON["RV_K"]; next }
    $3 == "-" && (($2 "/") in l) { print $1 "\t" $2 "\t" l[$2 "/"]; print $2 > ENVIRON["RV_K"]; next }
    { print }' "$w" "$3" "$1" > "$4" || return 1
  [ -f "$4.keep" ] || : > "$4.keep"
}
# READSET_UNIVERSE_END

# The deriver lands under a different parent on each side (install.sh copies core/scripts/<x>
# to scripts/ai-dlc/<x>), so both are probed and neither is assumed. Consumer layout first,
# the order every two-layout resolver here uses. Prints nothing when neither exists.
readset_deriver_path() {
  local c
  for c in scripts/ai-dlc/derive-fixture-readsets.sh core/scripts/derive-fixture-readsets.sh; do
    [ -f "$c" ] && { printf '%s' "$c"; return 0; }
  done
  return 0
}

# THE LOCAL MAP. After a GREEN suite this hook starts one detached, unprivileged trace of the
# fixtures that have no committed row and no valid local row (readset_live_trace, below), and the
# deriver records each clean trace here, under the git dir, never in the tree. `AI_DLC_READSET_LIVE_TRACE=0`
# turns the trace off; the rows already recorded are still read.
READSET_LOCAL="$GITDIR/ai-dlc-fixture-readsets.local"

# A LOCAL ROW SET IS VALID ONLY WHILE EVERY PATH IT RECORDED HASHES AS IT DID IN THE TRACE COPY, AND
# THE DERIVER IS THE ONE THAT TRACED IT. Read off THIS run's `$out/.now`, so a set whose input moved
# is ignored on the very push that moved it: the fixture is unmapped, runs, and is re-traced. A path
# outside the manifest's universe (`.git/**`, the content key's excluded tops, an ignored path) is
# not compared -- no change there can reach `.changed` either, for committed rows or local ones. A
# DIRECTORY row carries `#listing:<sha>` (the deriver's readset_dir_values, the committed digest's own
# substitution) and stays valid while this run's listing of that directory matches it; a `-` row is an
# ABSENT name and stays valid while nothing of that name -- file or directory -- exists. A `-` row for
# a path that is now a directory is stale: the trace never saw its listing.
# Writes `$out/.local.valid` (`<fx>\t<path>`), `$out/.trace` (the dispatch-list fixtures with no
# committed row and no valid local row) and `$out/.trace.held` (those of them discarded three times
# in a row on their current key, which are not re-traced until the key changes).
readset_local_validate() { # <list> <out>
  local list="$1" out="$2" dv dsha="" rc_map="$READSET_MAP" rl_loc="$READSET_LOCAL" d b k
  : > "$out/.local.valid"; : > "$out/.trace"; : > "$out/.trace.held"
  dv="$(readset_deriver_path)"
  [ -n "$dv" ] && dsha="$(shasum -a 256 -- "$dv" 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f1)"
  [ -s "$rc_map" ] || rc_map=/dev/null
  [ -s "$rl_loc" ] || rl_loc=/dev/null
  if [ -n "$dsha" ] && [ -s "$out/.now" ] && [ "$rl_loc" != /dev/null ]; then
    if [ ! -s "$out/.ls.now" ]; then cut -f1 "$out/.now" > "$out/.is"; readset_listings "$out/.is" "$out/.ls.now"; fi
    RL_C="$rc_map" RL_P="$out/.paths" RL_N="$out/.now" RL_L="$out/.ls.now" awk -F'\t' -v dsha="$dsha" '
      FILENAME == ENVIRON["RL_C"] { if ($0 !~ /^#/) com[$1] = 1; next }
      FILENAME == ENVIRON["RL_P"] { inu[$0] = 1; next }
      FILENAME == ENVIRON["RL_N"] { now[$1] = $2; next }
      FILENAME == ENVIRON["RL_L"] { now[substr($1, 1, length($1) - 1)] = $2; next }
      $2 == "#deriver" { dok[$1] = ($3 == dsha); next }
      substr($2, 1, 1) == "#" { next }
      { f = $1; seen[f] = 1; rows[f] = rows[f] $2 "\n"
        if ($2 in inu) {
          if ($3 == "-") { if ($2 in now) bad[f] = 1 }
          else if (!(($2 in now) && now[$2] == $3)) bad[f] = 1
        } }
      END { for (f in seen) if ((f in dok) && dok[f] && !(f in bad)) {
              n = split(rows[f], r, "\n"); for (i = 1; i < n; i++) print f "\t" r[i] } }' \
      "$rc_map" "$out/.paths" "$out/.now" "$out/.ls.now" "$rl_loc" | sort -u > "$out/.local.valid"
  fi
  # A COMMITTED ROW SET WHOSE DIGEST MATCHES THIS TREE IS VALID TOO, local map or none, and its rows are
  # written beside the valid local ones -- for a fixture that has none -- so a stale record clears on it
  # exactly as on a local trace. They ARE the committed rows, so selection does not move.
  readset_committed_digests "$out"
  if [ -s "$out/.cdig" ]; then
    RL_D="$out/.cdig" RL_V="$out/.local.valid" awk -F'\t' '
      FILENAME == ENVIRON["RL_D"] { if ($2 == "match") m[$1] = 1; next }
      FILENAME == ENVIRON["RL_V"] { v[$1] = 1; next }
      /^#/ { next }
      ($1 in m) && !($1 in v) && $2 != "" { print $1 "\t" $2 }' "$out/.cdig" "$out/.local.valid" "$rc_map" > "$out/.cdig.valid"
    sort -u "$out/.local.valid" "$out/.cdig.valid" > "$out/.local.valid.t" && mv "$out/.local.valid.t" "$out/.local.valid"
  fi
  readset_rows "$out" rows | cut -f1 | sort -u > "$out/.mapped.all"
  # A fixture carrying `inputs.decl` has no committed rows and needs none: its declaration is its read set
  # (readset_declared), so it is MAPPED here, before the trace queue below and the unmapped report in
  # apply_readset_skip read this file. Builtin tests only: an undeclared suite pays no fork for this.
  while IFS= read -r d; do
    b="${d%/}"; b="${b##*/}"
    [ -f "${FXROOT}${b}/inputs.decl" ] && printf '%s\n' "$b" >> "$out/.mapped.all"
  done < "$list"
  readset_unmapped "$list" "$out/.mapped.all" > "$out/.unm"
  while IFS= read -r b; do readset_trace_add "$out" "$b" "$dsha"; done < "$out/.unm"
}
# The dispatch list's fixture names (`<dir>/` -> its last component) absent from <mapped>, in list order, one awk.
readset_unmapped() { # <list> <mapped>
  M="$2" awk 'BEGIN { while ((getline l < ENVIRON["M"]) > 0) m[l] = 1 }
    { b = $0; sub(/\/$/, "", b); sub(/.*\//, "", b); if (!(b in m)) print b }' "$1"
}

# Queue <fixture> for the post-green trace, or hold it when its last three traces were discarded on
# the current run.sh and deriver. Unmapped fixtures come here from readset_local_validate, stale
# ones from apply_readset_skip.
readset_trace_add() { # <out> <fixture> <deriver sha>
  local k rl="$READSET_LOCAL"
  [ -s "$rl" ] || rl=/dev/null
  k="$(awk -F'\t' -v p="${FXROOT}$2/run.sh" '$1 == p { print $2 }' "$1/.now")"
  if awk -F'\t' -v f="$2" -v k="${k:--}:$3" '$1 == f && $2 == "#discards" && $3 >= 3 && $4 == k { h = 1 } END { exit !h }' "$rl"; then
    printf '%s\n' "$2" >> "$1/.trace.held"
  else
    printf '%s\n' "$2" >> "$1/.trace"
  fi
}

# READSET_LOCK_BEGIN
# The three lock helpers below are SOURCED by derive-fixture-readsets.sh for its trace-window slot, so the
# slot's writer and its stale test are this hook's own, never a restatement. Nothing else between the
# sentinels: the deriver sources the whole span.
# THE START TIME OF A PID, ONE NORMALISED STRING, for the live-trace lock. The writer and the reader
# both call this, so the two sides cannot disagree on locale, zone or column padding: `ps` runs
# under LC_ALL=C TZ=UTC0 and its words are re-joined by single spaces. Prints nothing and returns 1
# when neither source can read it (no such pid, or no usable `ps` and no fallback).
# THE FALLBACK IS THE SAME KERNEL FIELD, READ WITHOUT A SETUID BINARY (BL-686). `/bin/ps` is setuid
# root and `sandbox-exec` refuses to exec it (exit 126), so under a sandboxed harness the writers
# below recorded an EMPTY start line and every reader judged the lock on pid and age alone -- the
# reused-pid window this helper exists to close. When `ps` fails or prints nothing, the stock perl
# reads the pid's kinfo_proc through sysctl(KERN_PROC_PID) and prints its p_starttime with gmtime,
# which is `ps -o lstart=` under LC_ALL=C TZ=UTC0 word for word. macOS only ($^O); anywhere else,
# or for a dead pid (sysctl answers with zero length), it prints nothing and the answer is as before.
readset_pid_start() { # <pid>
  local x
  x="$(LC_ALL=C TZ=UTC0 ps -o lstart= -p "$1" 2>/dev/null)" || x=""
  [ -n "$x" ] || x="$(perl -e 'exit 1 unless $^O eq "darwin" && $ARGV[0] =~ /^[1-9][0-9]*$/; my $m = pack("i4", 1, 14, 1, $ARGV[0]); my $b = "\0" x 1024; my $l = pack("Q", 1024); syscall(202, $m, 4, $b, $l, 0, 0) == 0 or exit 1; my ($s) = unpack("q", $b); exit 1 unless unpack("Q", $l) > 0 && $s > 0; print scalar gmtime $s' "$1" 2>/dev/null)" || x=""
  set -- $x
  [ -n "$*" ] || return 1
  printf '%s' "$*"
}

# WRITE THE LIVE-TRACE LOCK'S PID FILE, the one writer both lock takers call. Line 1 `<pid> <epoch>`,
# line 2 the pid's start time. When neither `ps` nor the perl fallback can read it (BL-686: a sandbox
# refusing the setuid /bin/ps, on a host with no perl), it NEVER writes an empty line 2: it writes the
# two-field lock an older hook wrote, which readset_lock_stale judges HELD on pid and age alone -- the
# reader's own announce-and-hold -- and it says so on stderr.
readset_lock_write() { # <lock dir> <pid>
  local s
  if s="$(readset_pid_start "$2")"; then
    printf '%s %s\n%s\n' "$2" "$(date +%s)" "$s" > "$1/pid" 2>/dev/null
  else
    printf '%s %s\n' "$2" "$(date +%s)" > "$1/pid" 2>/dev/null
    printf '   ..    read-set lock: could not read the start time of pid %s (ps refused or printed nothing, and no perl fallback) -- the lock records no start line and is judged HELD on pid and age alone\n' "$2" >&2
  fi
}

# IS THE LIVE-TRACE LOCK STALE? `$lk/pid` is line 1 `<pid> <epoch>` (the only line an older hook
# writes or reads) and line 2 the start time of that pid as readset_pid_start printed it when the
# lock was taken. The pid is the detached trace subshell's `$!`, never `$$`. Decided in this order:
#   (1) a malformed pid or epoch                                   -> stale
#   (2) `kill -0` on the pid fails                                 -> stale
#   (3) the epoch is more than 6h old                              -> stale
#   (4) no start line (a lock taken by an older hook)              -> held, judged as before
#   (5) the pid's start time cannot be read now                    -> held, and it says so
#   (6) the pid's start time differs from the recorded one         -> stale (the pid was reused)
#   otherwise                                                      -> held
# (5) is reached only after `kill -0` succeeded, so an empty `ps` there is a `ps` failure or a pid
# that died in the gap. HELD with the announcement is right for both and the next push re-judges;
# do not turn it into stale, because a false stale lets two traces run at once. A lock dir with no
# pid file is stale once older than 60s (a launcher killed between `mkdir` and writing the pid). An
# EMPTY pid file is judged the same way, never as malformed: it is a writer between the redirect's
# truncate and its write, and reading it as stale let a concurrent push steal a live lock.
# Never a process-table grep: `kill -0` on the recorded pid is the liveness test.
readset_lock_stale() { # <lock dir>
  local lk="$1" p="" e="" now
  local s="" c=""
  if [ -s "$lk/pid" ]; then
    { read -r p e; read -r s; } < "$lk/pid" 2>/dev/null
    case "$p" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 0 ;; esac
    case "$e" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 0 ;; esac
    kill -0 "$p" 2>/dev/null || return 0
    now="$(date +%s)"
    [ $(( now - e )) -gt 21600 ] && return 0
    set -- $s; s="$*"
    [ -n "$s" ] || return 1
    if ! c="$(readset_pid_start "$p")"; then
      printf '   ..    read-set live trace: could not read the start time of lock pid %s (ps failed or printed nothing) -- the lock is judged HELD on pid and age alone\n' "$p"
      return 1
    fi
    [ "$c" != "$s" ] && return 0
    return 1
  fi
  [ -n "$(find "$lk" -maxdepth 0 -mmin +1 2>/dev/null)" ] && return 0
  return 1
}
# READSET_LOCK_END

# AFTER A GREEN SUITE, START ONE DETACHED TRACE OF THE UNMAPPED FIXTURES THAT RAN. The push is never
# delayed: every descriptor goes to a log under the git dir, so git has nothing to wait on, and the
# durations, the pole and the verified record were all written before this runs and are never
# touched by it. A fixture's normal-run output is stashed beside the local map first, because the
# deriver discards a trace whose verdict lines differ from the run that passed. Under a NON-blocking
# lock: if another trace holds it this push starts none and says so. Every precondition that fails
# is ONE line naming it, and the fixtures stay unmapped -- the behaviour before this existed.
readset_live_trace() { # <out>
  local out="$1" b names="" n=0 dv why="" lk tr logf tp cl
  [ "${AI_DLC_READSET_LIVE_TRACE:-1}" = 0 ] && return 0
  if [ -s "$out/.trace.held" ]; then
    printf '   ..    read-set live trace: NOT re-tracing %s -- discarded 3 times in a row on the same run.sh and deriver; unmapped until one of them changes (see %s.log)\n' \
      "$(tr '\n' ' ' < "$out/.trace.held" | sed 's/ $//')" "$READSET_LOCAL"
  fi
  [ -s "$out/.trace" ] || return 0
  while IFS= read -r b; do
    [ -n "$b" ] && [ "$(cat "$out/$b" 2>/dev/null)" = ok ] || continue
    names="${names}${names:+ }$b"; n=$((n + 1))
  done < "$out/.trace"
  [ "$n" -gt 0 ] || return 0
  dv="$(readset_deriver_path)"
  [ -n "$dv" ] || why="no derive-fixture-readsets.sh in either layout"
  command -v sandbox-exec >/dev/null 2>&1 || why="${why:+$why; }no sandbox-exec"
  [ -x /usr/bin/log ] || why="${why:+$why; }no /usr/bin/log"
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || why="${why:+$why; }no python3"
  [ "$(git rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null)" = "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)" ] \
    || why="${why:+$why; }a linked worktree (the deriver traces only the main checkout)"
  if [ -n "$why" ]; then
    printf '   ..    read-set live trace: skipped (%s); %s fixture(s) stay unmapped: %s\n' "$why" "$n" "$names"
    return 0
  fi
  lk="$READSET_LOCAL.lock"
  if ! mkdir "$lk" 2>/dev/null; then
    if readset_lock_stale "$lk"; then rm -f "$lk/pid"; rmdir "$lk" 2>/dev/null; fi
    if ! mkdir "$lk" 2>/dev/null; then
      printf '   ..    read-set live trace: another trace holds %s -- none started; %s fixture(s) stay unmapped this push\n' "$lk" "$n"
      return 0
    fi
  fi
  mkdir -p "$READSET_LOCAL.logs" 2>/dev/null
  for b in $names; do cp "$out/.log/$b" "$READSET_LOCAL.logs/$b" 2>/dev/null; done
  tr="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}"; tr="${tr%/}/ai-dlc-readset-live.$$"
  logf="$READSET_LOCAL.log"
  cl="$(readset_trace_ceiling "$names")"
  # THE STATUS FILE, `<local map>.status`, IS WRITTEN BY THE TRACE SUBSHELL ALONE, twice, each time
  # whole (temp + mv): `start`, `pid`, `list`, `deriver`, `ceiling` once the deriver is launched, and
  # the same plus `exit` and `finish` when the deriver returns -- before the lock is released, so a
  # reader that finds the lock gone finds the exit too, or knows the trace died. Its pid is read from
  # inside it (`$PPID` of a child it execs), the pid the lock records as `$!`. `exit timeout` is a
  # trace the ceiling killed. readset_trace_report reads it on the next push.
  #
  # THE CEILING, AND WHY THE KILL IS AIMED WHERE IT IS. A consumer's detached trace outlived its push
  # by 42 minutes, holding one `log stream` per fixture, with nothing that could end it. Two process
  # groups, each made by `set -m` around exactly one launch: the subshell is its own group (pgid
  # `$tp`, the lock's pid), and inside it the deriver is a BARE background command, so its pid `$dp` is
  # the deriver's own `$$` and the leader of a second group holding every `log stream` and sandboxed
  # fixture it starts. The watchdog sits in group `$tp`; at the ceiling it sends TERM, then KILL after
  # a 5s grace, to `-$dp` and nowhere else. Measured on bash 3.2: a KILL to `-$tp` kills this subshell,
  # so nothing writes the exit or releases the lock; and `trap '' TERM` here would be inherited as
  # SIG_IGN by the deriver, which then survives the TERM. A TERM to `$dp` alone leaves its children
  # running -- the orphan this exists to end. After a kill the deriver's EXIT trap never ran, so this
  # subshell removes the trace root and the deriver's map temp itself, by their literal names. Rows the
  # deriver finished before the kill stand (it writes the map after every fixture); the fixture in
  # progress gets none and stays unmapped. On a normal finish the watchdog is stopped by its pid and
  # its `sleep` is swept by a TERM to group `$tp`, so that group empties once this subshell exits, just after it
  # releases the lock -- not at the release itself, which a reader must not assume. The deriver is
  # outside group `$tp`, so a hand's `kill -- -<lock pid>` would leave it running with no ceiling:
  # the subshell's TERM trap (a handler, never SIG_IGN, so the deriver does not inherit it) forwards
  # that TERM to `-$dp` and then waits the deriver out before writing the exit.
  set -m
  ( trap '' HUP
    set +m
    sp="$(exec sh -c 'printf %s "$PPID"')"; t0="$(date +%s)"
    # THE LOCK'S PID FILE IS WRITTEN HERE, by the process that releases the lock, so the write can never land
    # after the release and overwrite a later holder's pid with this one's. Until it lands the lock dir has no
    # pid file, which readset_lock_stale judges held while the dir is under a minute old.
    readset_lock_write "$lk" "$sp"
    set -m
    AI_DLC_READSET_TRACE_ROOT="$tr" bash "$dv" --list "$names" --tracer sandbox --local-map "$READSET_LOCAL" &
    dp=$!
    set +m
    printf 'start %s\npid %s\nlist %s\nderiver %s\nceiling %s\n' "$t0" "$sp" "$names" "$dp" "$cl" > "$READSET_LOCAL.status.tmp.$sp" 2>/dev/null \
      && mv "$READSET_LOCAL.status.tmp.$sp" "$READSET_LOCAL.status" 2>/dev/null
    # THE VERDICT IS THE WATCHDOG'S EXIT STATUS: 3 only when its own TERM to the deriver's group was
    # delivered. Once its sleep has run out it ignores TERM, so the stop sent below cannot cut a kill
    # in progress short and read it as a normal finish; a stop that lands during the sleep exits 0.
    # The subshell sends no signal to `-$dp` after it has reaped `$dp`: that group id may be reused, so
    # its forwarding trap is replaced by an ignore once the reap is done, which also keeps a late TERM
    # from interrupting `wait "$wp"` and losing the watchdog's verdict.
    # THE WATCHDOG HAS NO TERM TRAP. A stop kills it by the default action, so no handler exists to be lost
    # or to run late. Its `sleep` stays in group `$sp` and is swept by the TERM to `-$sp` below, sent once
    # `$wp` is reaped and while this subshell ignores TERM. The lock is released last, by
    # `rm -f "$lk/pid"; rmdir "$lk"`; the pid file is this subshell's own (written at its start), so nothing
    # else writes into `$lk` and `rmdir` cannot find it occupied.
    (
      sleep "$cl" & s=$!; wait "$s"
      trap '' TERM
      kill -TERM -- "-$dp" 2>/dev/null || exit 0
      g=0; while kill -0 -- "-$dp" 2>/dev/null && [ "$g" -lt 50 ]; do sleep 0.1; g=$((g + 1)); done
      kill -KILL -- "-$dp" 2>/dev/null
      exit 3
    ) &
    wp=$!
    trap 'kill -TERM -- "-$dp" 2>/dev/null' TERM
    wait "$dp"; rc=$?
    while kill -0 "$dp" 2>/dev/null; do wait "$dp"; rc=$?; done
    trap '' TERM
    kill "$wp" 2>/dev/null
    wait "$wp" 2>/dev/null; [ "$?" = 3 ] && rc=timeout
    kill -TERM -- "-$sp" 2>/dev/null
    case "$tr" in */ai-dlc-readset-live.[0-9]*) rm -rf "$tr" ;; esac
    rm -f "$READSET_LOCAL.tmp.$dp"
    printf 'start %s\npid %s\nlist %s\nderiver %s\nceiling %s\nexit %s\nfinish %s\n' "$t0" "$sp" "$names" "$dp" "$cl" "$rc" "$(date +%s)" > "$READSET_LOCAL.status.tmp.$sp" 2>/dev/null \
      && mv "$READSET_LOCAL.status.tmp.$sp" "$READSET_LOCAL.status" 2>/dev/null
    rm -f "$lk/pid"; rmdir "$lk" 2>/dev/null
  ) </dev/null >"$logf" 2>&1 &
  tp="$!"
  set +m
  printf '   ..    read-set live trace: started detached for %s unmapped fixture(s) (%s) -> %s; ceiling %ss\n' "$n" "$names" "$logf" "$cl"
  return 0
}

# THE TRACE'S WALL-CLOCK CEILING, in seconds: 3 x the sum of the listed fixtures' recorded durations
# (DURATIONS_RECORD; a fixture with no row counts 300s), floored at 600s and capped at 20000s.
# k = 3 because a trace costs about what its fixtures cost: measured on a consumer, 16 fixtures with
# 649s of recorded durations traced in 371s, and a 42-fixture list of 2424s was still working at
# 2508s -- so a working trace finishes inside 3x and only a hung one reaches it. This is a HANG guard:
# that 42-fixture list's ceiling is 7272s, so it would not have ended that slow, working trace. The cap is strictly
# below the lock's 6h (21600s) staleness epoch in readset_lock_stale, less the 5s kill grace: a live
# trace past that reads stale and a second one starts beside it. AI_DLC_READSET_TRACE_CEILING
# (whole seconds, >= 1) replaces the derived figure and is held to the same cap.
readset_trace_ceiling() { # <space-separated fixture names>
  local c dr=/dev/null
  [ -s "$DURATIONS_RECORD" ] && dr="$DURATIONS_RECORD"
  c="$(awk -v L="$1" 'BEGIN { n = split(L, a, " ") }
    NF == 2 && $2 ~ /^[0-9]+$/ { d[$1] = $2 }
    END { s = 0; for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) s += ((a[i] in d) ? d[a[i]] : 300); print 3 * s }' "$dr" 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$c" in ''|*[!0-9]*) c=600 ;; esac
  [ "$c" -lt 600 ] && c=600
  case "${AI_DLC_READSET_TRACE_CEILING:-}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ;; ??????*) c=20000 ;; *) [ "$((10#$AI_DLC_READSET_TRACE_CEILING))" -ge 1 ] && c="$((10#$AI_DLC_READSET_TRACE_CEILING))" ;; esac
  [ "$c" -gt 20000 ] && c=20000
  printf '%s' "$c"
}

# THE IN-POOL TRACE. An undeclared fixture queued in `$out/.trace` is run by its pool worker through
# `derive-fixture-readsets.sh --in-pool`, in an APFS clone of the tree, so the run that gives the push its
# verdict is the run that is traced -- readset_live_trace's second run after the suite is gone for it. Every
# omission rule of the detached trace holds (drop notices, the loss canary, the unread control, the dirty
# delta, TRIP, the driver), so a lossy window discards and never maps a smaller set; a TRIP re-runs the
# fixture untraced and takes THAT verdict. Any failure to trace leaves the fixture to a plain run and to the
# detached trace, exactly as before. readset_pool_trace_prep sets PT_ROOT, PT_LIST and PT_DV for the
# worker; readset_pool_trace_merge is the ONE writer of the local map afterwards, under the live trace's lock.
# A LINKED WORKTREE TRACES TOO: the deriver builds the clone's `.git` standalone from the common dir and the
# worktree's private gitdir, and the local map sits under `--git-common-dir`, the one file every worktree reads.
readset_pool_trace_prep() { # <out>
  local out="$1" b why=""
  PT_ROOT=""; PT_LIST=""; PT_DV=""
  [ "${AI_DLC_READSET_LIVE_TRACE:-1}" = 0 ] && return 0
  [ -s "$out/.trace" ] || return 0
  while IFS= read -r b; do
    [ -n "$b" ] && [ ! -f "${FXROOT}${b}/inputs.decl" ] || continue
    PT_LIST="${PT_LIST}${PT_LIST:+ }$b"
  done < "$out/.trace"
  [ -n "$PT_LIST" ] || return 0
  PT_DV="$(readset_deriver_path)"
  [ -n "$PT_DV" ] || why="no derive-fixture-readsets.sh in either layout"
  command -v sandbox-exec >/dev/null 2>&1 || why="${why:+$why; }no sandbox-exec"
  [ -x /usr/bin/log ] || why="${why:+$why; }no /usr/bin/log"
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || why="${why:+$why; }no python3"
  if [ -n "$why" ]; then
    printf '   ..    read-set in-pool trace: skipped (%s); %s run plainly: %s\n' "$why" "$(printf '%s\n' $PT_LIST | grep -c .)" "$PT_LIST"
    PT_LIST=""; PT_DV=""
    return 0
  fi
  mkdir -p "$out/.lt" || { PT_LIST=""; PT_DV=""; return 0; }
  PT_ROOT="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}"; PT_ROOT="${PT_ROOT%/}/ai-dlc-readset-pool.$$"
  printf '   ..    read-set in-pool trace: %s fixture(s) traced in the pool: %s\n' "$(printf '%s\n' $PT_LIST | grep -c .)" "$PT_LIST"
}
readset_pool_trace_merge() { # <out>
  local out="$1" lk b n=0 kept="" disc="" rest
  [ -n "${PT_ROOT:-}" ] && [ -d "$out/.lt" ] || return 0
  for b in $PT_LIST; do
    [ -f "$out/.lt/$b/rows" ] && kept="${kept}${kept:+ }$b"
    [ -f "$out/.lt/$b/discard" ] && disc="${disc}${disc:+ }$b"
    [ -f "$out/.lt/$b/rows" ] || [ -f "$out/.lt/$b/discard" ] && n=$((n + 1))
  done
  # A fixture the pool TRACED, whatever came of it, leaves the detached trace's queue: its run was this one.
  rest="$(while IFS= read -r b; do [ -f "$out/.lt/$b/rc" ] || printf '%s\n' "$b"; done < "$out/.trace")"
  printf '%s\n' "$rest" | grep . > "$out/.trace.t"; mv "$out/.trace.t" "$out/.trace"
  [ "$n" -gt 0 ] || return 0
  lk="$READSET_LOCAL.lock"
  if ! mkdir "$lk" 2>/dev/null; then
    if readset_lock_stale "$lk"; then rm -f "$lk/pid"; rmdir "$lk" 2>/dev/null; fi
    if ! mkdir "$lk" 2>/dev/null; then
      printf '   ..    read-set in-pool trace: another trace holds %s -- %s in-pool trace(s) not recorded this push\n' "$lk" "$n"
      return 0
    fi
  fi
  readset_lock_write "$lk" "$$"
  if bash "$PT_DV" --merge-pool "$out/.lt" --local-map "$READSET_LOCAL" > "$out/.lt.merge" 2>&1; then
    printf '   ..    read-set in-pool trace: recorded %s%s\n' "${kept:-none}" "${disc:+; discarded: $disc}"
  else
    printf '   ..    read-set in-pool trace: the merge refused, nothing recorded: %s\n' "$(tail -1 "$out/.lt.merge")"
  fi
  rm -f "$lk/pid"; rmdir "$lk" 2>/dev/null
}

readset_when() { # <epoch>: local wall-clock time, or the bare epoch where neither date spelling works
  date -r "$1" '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S' 2>/dev/null || date -d "@$1" '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S' 2>/dev/null || printf 'epoch %s' "$1"
}

# THE READ-SET TRACE REPORT, PRINTED ON EVERY PUSH BEFORE THE SUITE. The detached trace's outcomes reach
# only the local map's `#discards` rows, its log and its status file, and before this nothing read
# them back: a fixture was named once, on the push its third discard was recorded. Every line opens
# with `trace-report <field>:`. Read from, and never written to:
#   <local map>.status   written by the trace subshell (readset_live_trace): start, pid, list, exit
#   <local map>.lock/pid the lock; held means the trace is running now
#   <local map>.log      the last trace's own output, truncated when the NEXT trace starts. That start
#                        is in readset_live_trace, after the suite, so this report has always read the
#                        log and status of the trace before it -- the ordering is the guarantee.
#   <local map>          the `#discards` rows: `<fx> #discards <n> <run.sh sha>:<deriver sha> <why>`
#   <out>/.kdec .kl .mapped.all   this push's decisions; absent where no keys were built
# HOLD SEMANTICS: a fixture whose last three traces were discarded on its CURRENT run.sh and deriver
# is not re-traced, and runs on every push. It is named on every push until its key moves; `n` counts
# consecutive discards on one key, so a release that edits the deriver resets every count and clears
# every held line. A finished trace that recorded nothing, or one that died, is a WARN line.
readset_trace_report() { # <out, or empty where this push built no keys>
  local out="${1:-}" rl="$READSET_LOCAL" lg="$READSET_LOCAL.log" st="$READSET_LOCAL.status" lk="$READSET_LOCAL.lock"
  local P='   ..    trace-report' dv dsha="-" k v s_start="" s_pid="" s_list="" s_exit="" s_fin="" s_ceil="" s_dp="" now
  local lp="" le="" run=0 died=0 state ck="" kd=/dev/null kl=/dev/null km=/dev/null why=""
  now="$(date +%s)"
  if [ -f "$st" ]; then
    while read -r k v; do
      case "$k" in start) s_start="$v" ;; pid) s_pid="$v" ;; list) s_list="$v" ;; exit) s_exit="$v" ;; finish) s_fin="$v" ;; ceiling) s_ceil="$v" ;; deriver) s_dp="$v" ;; esac
    done < "$st"
  fi
  case "$s_start" in ''|*[!0-9]*) s_start="" ;; esac
  case "$s_fin" in ''|*[!0-9]*) s_fin="$s_start" ;; esac
  if [ -d "$lk" ] && ! readset_lock_stale "$lk" >/dev/null; then
    run=1
    [ -f "$lk/pid" ] && read -r lp le < "$lk/pid"
    case "$le" in ''|*[!0-9]*) le="$now" ;; esac
  fi
  # A TIMED-OUT TRACE IS NOT A FINISH AND NOT A CRASH: the ceiling killed the deriver's process group,
  # the fixtures it had not finished stay unmapped, and the WARN below names it once. The `deriver` and
  # `ceiling` lines are absent from a status file an older hook wrote, and from one read in the gap
  # before the subshell rewrites it with them; neither is required here.
  if [ "$s_exit" = timeout ]; then
    state="TIMED OUT at the ${s_ceil:-?}s ceiling, at $(readset_when "${s_fin:-0}") ($(( ${s_fin:-0} - ${s_start:-0} ))s); deriver group ${s_dp:-?} killed"
  elif [ -n "$s_exit" ]; then
    state="finished, exit $s_exit, at $(readset_when "${s_fin:-0}") ($(( ${s_fin:-0} - ${s_start:-0} ))s)"
  elif [ -n "$s_start" ] && [ "$run" = 1 ] && [ "$lp" = "$s_pid" ]; then
    state="RUNNING since $(readset_when "$s_start") ($(( now - s_start ))s), pid $s_pid"
  elif [ -n "$s_start" ]; then
    state="DIED without writing its exit (pid $s_pid no longer holds the lock and no exit was recorded)"; died=1
  elif [ "$run" = 1 ]; then
    state="RUNNING since $(readset_when "${le:-0}") ($(( now - ${le:-0} ))s), pid ${lp:-?}; no status file (started by a hook older than the status file)"
  else
    state="none recorded (no status file at $st)"
  fi
  if [ -n "$s_start" ]; then
    printf '%s last trace: started %s, %s fixture(s); %s\n' "$P" "$(readset_when "$s_start")" "$(set -- $s_list; echo $#)" "$state"
  else
    printf '%s last trace: %s\n' "$P" "$state"
  fi
  if [ "$run" = 1 ] && [ -n "$s_start" ] && [ "$lp" != "$s_pid" ]; then
    printf '%s lock: another trace holds %s (pid %s since %s); the status file is not its own\n' "$P" "$lk" "${lp:-?}" "$(readset_when "$le")"
  fi
  # A DERIVER GROUP THAT NOTHING WILL END. The deriver leads its own process group, outside the lock's,
  # so a `kill -9` of the trace subshell leaves it and its `log stream` children running with no
  # ceiling, and a hung one runs past the ceiling if the watchdog itself was lost. Keyed on what the
  # status file records: a `deriver` pgid, no `exit`, that group still alive, and EITHER the status pid
  # is gone OR the trace has run past its `ceiling` plus the kill grace. One line, with the command.
  case "$s_dp" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ;; *)
    if [ -z "$s_exit" ] && [ -n "$s_start" ] && kill -0 -- "-$s_dp" 2>/dev/null; then
      why=""
      case "$s_pid" in ''|*[!0-9]*) why="its trace subshell is unrecorded" ;; *) kill -0 "$s_pid" 2>/dev/null || why="its trace subshell (pid $s_pid) is gone" ;; esac
      case "$s_ceil" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ;; *) [ -z "$why" ] && [ $(( now - s_start )) -gt $(( s_ceil + 5 )) ] && why="it has run $(( now - s_start ))s, past its ${s_ceil}s ceiling" ;; esac
      [ -n "$why" ] && printf '   WARN  trace-report warning: the last trace'"'"'s deriver group %s is still running and %s; stop it with: kill -TERM -- -%s\n' "$s_dp" "$why" "$s_dp"
    fi ;;
  esac
  dv="$(readset_deriver_path)"
  [ -n "$dv" ] && dsha="$(shasum -a 256 -- "$dv" 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f1)"
  if [ -s "$rl" ]; then
    ck="$(awk -F'\t' -v r="$FXROOT" '$2 == "#discards" { print r $1 "/run.sh" }' "$rl" | tr '\n' '\000' | xargs -0 shasum -a 256 -- 2>/dev/null)"
  else
    printf '%s local map: none at %s -- no trace has recorded a row or a discard in this clone\n' "$P" "$rl"
    rl=/dev/null
  fi
  [ -f "$lg" ] || lg=/dev/null
  if [ -n "$out" ] && [ -s "$out/.kdec" ] && [ -s "$out/.kl" ] && [ "$(grep -c . "$out/.kdec")" = "$(grep -c . "$out/.kl")" ]; then
    kd="$out/.kdec"; kl="$out/.kl"; [ -f "$out/.mapped.all" ] && km="$out/.mapped.all"
  fi
  RS_CK="$ck" RS_LIST="$s_list" RS_FIN="$s_exit" RS_DIED="$died" \
  RS_LG="$lg" RS_RL="$rl" RS_KD="$kd" RS_KL="$kl" RS_KM="$km" RS_LOGP="$READSET_LOCAL.log" \
  awk -F'\t' -v P="$P" -v r="$FXROOT" -v dsha="$dsha" '
    function cls(c) {
      if (index(c, "the stream dropped reports")) return "stream-drop"
      if (index(c, "VERDICT:") == 1) return "verdict"
      if (index(c, "LOSS CANARY:") == 1) return "loss-canary"
      if (index(c, "TRIP:") == 1) return "trip"
      if (index(c, "STAT LOSS:") == 1) return "stat-loss"
      if (c ~ /^fixture exited /) return "fixture-exit"
      if (c == "tracing never settled") return "never-settled"
      if (index(c, "no end sentinel")) return "no-end-sentinel"
      if (index(c, "UNREAD CONTROL")) return "unread-control"
      if (c == "empty read-set") return "empty-set"
      if (c ~ /^fixture wrote /) return "wrote-tree"
      if (index(c, "the set does not name its own")) return "no-own-run.sh"
      return "other" }
    function tally(why, arr,   n, part, i, c, u) {
      n = split(why, part, "; ")
      for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) { c = cls(part[i]); if (!(c in u)) { u[c] = 1; arr[c]++ } } }
    function show(arr,   i, s) { s = ""
      for (i = 1; i <= NC; i++) if (arr[C[i]] > 0) s = s (s == "" ? "" : ", ") C[i] " " arr[C[i]]
      return s == "" ? "none" : s }
    function names(arr,   f, s, n, a, i, j, t) { n = 0
      for (f in arr) a[++n] = f
      for (i = 2; i <= n; i++) { t = a[i]; for (j = i - 1; j >= 1 && a[j] > t; j--) a[j + 1] = a[j]; a[j + 1] = t }
      s = ""; for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) s = s (s == "" ? "" : " ") a[i]
      return s }
    BEGIN {
      NC = split("stream-drop verdict loss-canary trip stat-loss fixture-exit never-settled no-end-sentinel unread-control empty-set wrote-tree no-own-run.sh not-hashed other", C, " ")
      n = split(ENVIRON["RS_CK"], a, "\n")
      for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) { j = index(a[i], "  "); if (j) { p = substr(a[i], j + 2); RK[p] = substr(a[i], 1, j - 1) } }
      ng = split(ENVIRON["RS_LIST"], g, " ")
      # THE LAST TRACE, from its log. `<fx>  N paths` is a CLEAN trace, not a recorded one: a following
      # `could not hash <fx>...` line means its rows were never written.
      while ((getline l < ENVIRON["RS_LG"]) > 0) {
        if (index(l, "  could not hash ") == 1 && (q = index(l, "s set in the trace copy -- not recorded")) > 0) {
          NH[substr(l, 18, q - 19)] = 1; continue }
        if (substr(l, 1, 2) != "  " || substr(l, 3, 1) == " ") continue
        m = split(substr(l, 3), w, " ")
        if (m == 3 && w[3] == "paths" && w[2] ~ /^[0-9]+$/) { CLEAN[w[1]] = 1; seen[w[1]] = 1; continue }
        if (m >= 3 && w[2] == "OMITTED") { y = substr(l, 3); sub(/^[^ ]+ +OMITTED \(/, "", y); sub(/\) -- will always run$/, "", y)
          DROP[w[1]] = 1; seen[w[1]] = 1; tally(y, TC) } }
      for (f in NH) { TC["not-hashed"]++ }
      nrec = 0; for (f in CLEAN) if (!(f in NH)) nrec++
      ndrop = 0; for (f in DROP) ndrop++
      nnh = 0; for (f in NH) nnh++
      nseen = 0; for (f in seen) nseen++
      if (ng == 0) ng = nseen
      if (ENVIRON["RS_LG"] == "/dev/null") printf "%s last trace outcome: no trace log at %s\n", P, ENVIRON["RS_LOGP"]
      else printf "%s last trace outcome: %d given, %d recorded, %d discarded, %d traced cleanly but NOT recorded; causes %s\n", P, ng, nrec, ndrop, nnh, show(TC)
      # THE STANDING STATE, from the #discards rows of the local map.
      nd = 0
      while ((getline l < ENVIRON["RS_RL"]) > 0) {
        split(l, x, "\t"); if (x[2] != "#discards") continue
        nd++; f = x[1]; c = x[3] + 0; tally(x[5], DC)
        if (c >= 3) at3++; else if (c == 2) at2++; else at1++
        cur = ((r f "/run.sh") in RK ? RK[r f "/run.sh"] : "-") ":" dsha
        if (c >= 3 && x[4] == cur) HELD[f] = 1
        else if (c == 2 && x[4] == cur) TWO[f] = 1 }
      if (nd == 0) printf "%s discards: none recorded\n", P
      else printf "%s discards: %d fixture(s) carry a #discards row (at 1: %d, at 2: %d, at 3 or more: %d); causes %s\n", P, nd, at1, at2, at3, show(DC)
      s = names(TWO); printf "%s at 2 of 3: %s\n", P, (s == "" ? "none" : s " -- one more discard on the same key holds it")
      s = names(HELD)
      if (s == "") printf "%s held: none\n", P
      else printf "%s held: %s -- discarded 3 times in a row on its current run.sh and deriver, so it is NOT re-traced and runs on every push until one of them changes; a release that edits the deriver resets every #discards count\n", P, s
      s = names(NH); printf "%s not recorded: %s\n", P, (s == "" ? "none" : s " -- traced cleanly, then could not hash its set in the trace copy")
      # THIS PUSH: what runs because no trace has cleared it.
      if (ENVIRON["RS_KD"] == "/dev/null") printf "%s uncleared this push: unknown -- no fixture keys were built this push\n", P
      else {
        while ((getline l < ENVIRON["RS_KM"]) > 0) MAP[l] = 1
        ns = 0; nu = 0
        while ((getline l < ENVIRON["RS_KD"]) > 0) { split(l, x, "\t"); if (x[2] != "run") continue
          if (x[4] == "s") ns++; else if (!(x[1] in MAP)) nu++ }
        printf "%s uncleared this push: %d stale and %d unmapped fixture(s) run because no trace has cleared them\n", P, ns, nu }
      if (ENVIRON["RS_DIED"] == "1") printf "   WARN  trace-report warning: the last trace DIED without writing its exit -- nothing it was given can be trusted as recorded; see %s\n", ENVIRON["RS_LOGP"]
      else if (ENVIRON["RS_FIN"] == "timeout") printf "   WARN  trace-report warning: the last trace TIMED OUT at its wall-clock ceiling and was killed; %d of %d fixture(s) recorded, the rest stay unmapped (AI_DLC_READSET_TRACE_CEILING raises it); see %s\n", nrec, ng, ENVIRON["RS_LOGP"]
      else if (ENVIRON["RS_FIN"] != "" && ng > 0 && nrec == 0) {
        b = ""; bn = 0; for (i = 1; i <= NC; i++) if (TC[C[i]] > bn) { bn = TC[C[i]]; b = C[i] }
        printf "   WARN  trace-report warning: the last trace recorded NOTHING for the %d fixture(s) it was given; dominant cause %s (%d)\n", ng, (b == "" ? "unknown" : b), bn }
    }'
  return 0
}

# THE TOOLS, KEYED PER FIXTURE. One pass over the manifest's `.sh` files writes `<out>/.ftools`
# (`<file>\t<tool path>`): every word of a non-comment line that names a command in the TOOL DIRS below,
# the first hit winning. Derived that way, never hand-listed. `<out>/.tools` is `<tool path>\t<sha256>` for
# every tool named anywhere. F's tool keys are the tools its keyed files name, so a changed binary reruns
# only the fixtures whose files name it. Keyed on the binary's CONTENT: nothing is EXECUTED to read a
# version, because the derived name set includes commands such as `halt`, `yes` and `passwd`.
#
# THE TOOL DIRS ARE FIXED, NEVER THE INHERITED $PATH, so the key does not depend on who invoked the hook.
# `git push` prepends git's exec-path to a hook's PATH; `self-update-push.sh` (`exec "$HOOK"`), a shell
# and a fixture harness do not. Resolved on $PATH, `git` named a different binary per invoker, each
# invocation's records read the other's tool rows as changed, and a self-update push followed by a
# reconcile push reran nearly the whole suite on an unchanged tree. The list is git's REAL exec-path
# first, then READSET_TOOL_DIRS. The exec-path is read once per decision from /usr/bin/git with
# DEVELOPER_DIR and GIT_EXEC_PATH removed, so it names the binary behind the xcrun shim whatever the
# environment says -- the binary a fixture's `git` runs under `git push`, and the one the shim forwards
# to under any other invoker. The order of the dirs puts Homebrew ahead of the system, as a login PATH
# does, so `jq`, `python3` and `timeout` key the copies fixtures execute.
# A COMMAND FOUND ONLY OUTSIDE THESE DIRS -- node, npm or claude under ~/.nvm or ~/.local/bin -- IS NO
# LONGER KEYED, and that is deliberate: any key that depends on PATH reintroduces the defect. Both
# values are ASSIGNED here, so nothing inherited from the environment can set them; a fixture overrides
# them after sourcing this block.
# THE ESCAPE, AND WHAT IT COSTS. A fixture that must run node, npm, npx or claude declares it in
# tools.decl as `?name`. That tool is NOT keyed: no row is written for it, so which copy runs depends on
# the INVOKER's PATH while the key does not. The cost is that a changed node does not rerun the fixture;
# that is the price of not reintroducing a per-invoker key. `?name` is accepted only for a name in
# READSET_UNKEYED_TOOLS below, the single owner of that vocabulary: the hermetic runner sources this
# span and reads the same list. Any other `?name` is unresolved.
# READSET_TOOLS_BEGIN
READSET_TOOL_DIRS="/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"
READSET_UNKEYED_TOOLS="node npm npx claude"
READSET_TOOL_XP=""
readset_git_exec_path() {
  local g
  for g in /usr/bin/git /usr/local/bin/git /opt/homebrew/bin/git; do
    [ -x "$g" ] || continue
    /usr/bin/env -u DEVELOPER_DIR -u GIT_EXEC_PATH "$g" --exec-path 2>/dev/null
    return 0
  done
  return 0
}
# READSET_TOOLS_END
